# Declared exceptions for scripts/verify-name-in-path.sh — the personal-name-in-
# path detector. Anything not listed here that the detector finds is a failure.
#
# TWO KINDS OF ENTRY, exactly as `.hardcoded-paths-allow` defines them. The
# marker on the comment block directly above a path decides which:
#
#   # REASON:   <why this is demonstrably not a person>
#               -> justified; invisible in the summary beyond a count.
#   # BASELINE: <a REAL, KNOWN finding that is deliberately not fixed yet>
#               -> PRINTED LOUDLY on every run. Recorded DEBT, never a reason.
#
# A marker carries across a contiguous run of paths; a blank line ends the run.
# An unmarked entry is honoured AND named in an advisory warning, because an
# unexplained suppression is indistinguishable from a bluff.
#
# TWO SCOPES, and prefer the narrower one:
#
#   docs/thing.md        FILE-scoped   — pardons every finding in that file,
#                                        including ones that do not exist yet.
#   docs/thing.md:412    LINE-scoped   — pardons exactly that line.
#
# LINE SCOPE IS THE DEFAULT HERE, and that is a deliberate difference from the
# sibling allow-lists. This gate exists because a single line of a single file
# disclosed a real person. A file-scoped pardon on a file that once contained a
# name is precisely the mechanism that would hide the next one.
#
# THE COST OF LINE SCOPE IS STATED RATHER THAN DISCOVERED: a line number moves
# when the file above it changes — and for a path inside a submodule, an
# ordinary gitlink bump moves it. When that happens the entry goes STALE, the
# detector prints it as a stale allow entry, and the finding returns as a
# failure. That is not a defect. For a privacy gate a false red costs a minute
# of reading; a false green costs a permanent public disclosure.
#
# NEVER write a personal name in this file. Entries are PATHS and LINE NUMBERS.
# If you need to explain why something is not a person, describe the evidence
# and its location — do not quote the token.

# REASON: a SERVICE ACCOUNT, not a human, and the file itself proves it two
# lines earlier. `submodules/containers/pkg/emulator/Containerfile` line 73 is
#     useradd -m -u 1000 -s /bin/bash <account>
# and line 74 is `USER <account>`; line 75 is the `WORKDIR /home/<account>` that
# the detector matches. The account is named after the workload the image runs,
# it is created by the image, and the same token occurs 31× as a path component
# across the scanned corpus — measured 2026-09-02, and the opposite of the
# once-or-twice signature a leaked personal name produces.
#
# This is LINE-scoped on purpose. `submodules/containers` is a submodule this
# repository cannot edit (§11.4.29), so the entry cannot be removed by fixing
# the source; it can only be re-verified. A gitlink bump that moves line 75 will
# make this entry stale and turn the gate red until somebody looks again, which
# is the correct behaviour for a privacy gate reading a tree it does not own.
